window.BOOK = {"cover": {"series": "AN ONCHAIN EDUCATION SERIES", "title": "WALLY'S RWA TEXTBOOK", "sponsor": "CREATED BY RWA FOUNDATION", "handle": "@wallycollection", "blurb": ["Most RWA takes are a chart and a promise. This isn't that.", "Wally covers the vocabulary, the asset classes and how to tell when a chart is doing more work than the data behind it.", "No price calls. No hopium.", "Just what you need before you put money into anything with 'onchain' in the name."]}, "chapters": [{"n": 1, "id": "foundations", "name": "Foundations", "lessons": [{"id": "1.1", "page": 1, "title": "What Is an RWA?", "sub": "Real-world asset", "body": ["A real-world asset, or RWA, is an asset that exists outside a blockchain, such as a Treasury bill, a private loan, a building or a bar of gold, and is represented by a token onchain. The token records a claim on the asset or on the entity that holds it. The asset itself stays where it is, with the same value and the same risks."], "point": "Tokenization does not change what an asset is worth. It can change who can hold it, how fast it settles and what can be built on top of it.", "chapter": 1, "note": "a building. in a wallet. (sort of)", "t": 32.86}, {"id": "1.2", "page": 2, "title": "Tokenization", "sub": "From asset to token", "body": ["Tokenization means representing an asset, such as a US Treasury bill or a share of a private credit fund, as a token on a blockchain. A legal structure links the token to the asset, and the token becomes the record of who owns it. The asset does not change. What can change is how ownership moves: a token can settle within minutes, transfer outside banking hours where the rules allow it and be split into smaller units than the original instrument."], "point": "The RWA is still the same asset. Tokenization changes how ownership is recorded and how it moves.", "chapter": 1, "note": "same burrito. new tortilla.", "t": 41.43}, {"id": "1.3", "page": 3, "title": "Onchain", "sub": "Where the ownership record lives", "body": ["Onchain means the ownership record is kept on a blockchain, not only in an issuer's or administrator's internal database. The building, the loan or the bond stays where it is. What sits onchain is the record of who holds the claim, how much they hold and when it last moved. On a public blockchain anyone can check that record without asking the issuer. Whether the onchain record is also the legal record of ownership depends on how the product is set up."], "point": "Putting a record onchain does not improve the asset. It makes the ownership record easier to check and harder to change quietly.", "chapter": 1, "note": "nice try, eraser", "t": 50.0}, {"id": "1.4", "page": 4, "title": "Custody", "sub": "Who holds the underlying asset", "body": ["Custody is about who holds the underlying asset and on what terms. For a tokenized Treasury fund, a custodian bank holds the bills. For gold, a vault operator holds the bars. For a loan, someone holds the documents and collects the payments. The custody agreement says whether those assets are kept separate from the platform's own, who can move them and what happens to them if the issuer or the platform fails."], "point": "A token is only as reliable as the custody behind it. Check who holds the asset and what happens to it if the platform fails.", "chapter": 1, "note": "*gavel noises*", "t": 58.57}, {"id": "1.5", "page": 5, "title": "Transfer Agent", "sub": "Who keeps the official ownership records", "body": ["A transfer agent keeps the official ownership records of a security and helps enforce the restrictions on who can hold it. When a tokenized security changes hands, those records and restrictions decide whether the transfer is allowed, based on checks such as KYC, investor eligibility and jurisdiction.", "Some tokenized assets have no separate transfer agent. They build the same restrictions into the token itself, using an allowlist of approved wallets that is checked before every transfer. Either way, a token can move onchain in seconds. Legal validity depends on the law and the product's terms and ownership records."], "point": "A token can move in seconds. Whether the transfer is valid depends on the law, the product's terms and the ownership records, not on the blockchain alone.", "chapter": 1, "note": "bouncer energy", "t": 67.14}, {"id": "1.6", "page": 6, "title": "Total Value (vs TVL)", "sub": "Two numbers that often get treated as one", "body": ["Total value measures all the tokenized assets of a given type, wherever they sit: in wallets, with custodians or inside protocols. TVL, or total value locked, is narrower. It counts only the assets deposited into a protocol's smart contracts, for example as collateral or liquidity. A tokenized Treasury fund held in an investor's wallet counts toward total value but not toward any protocol's TVL. Mixing the two figures makes assets that are simply being held look as if they are being used."], "point": "Total value shows how much exists onchain. TVL shows how much is deposited in protocols. Check which one a chart is measuring.", "chapter": 1, "note": "that's not demand. that's a nap.", "t": 75.71}, {"id": "1.7", "page": 7, "title": "Issuance vs Distribution", "sub": "Creating tokens and getting them into many hands are different jobs", "body": ["Issuance is the creation of tokens. Once the issuer mints them, the supply exists. Distribution is about who ends up holding that supply. A product can be fully issued on day one while most of the tokens sit with the issuer, a market maker or a few early investors. The tokens have been issued, but holdings are still concentrated. The size of an issuance on its own is not evidence of demand."], "point": "Issuance tells you how many tokens exist. Distribution tells you how many holders there are and how much each one holds.", "chapter": 1, "note": "issued: 1B. holders: the founder's group chat", "t": 84.29}, {"id": "1.8", "page": 8, "title": "Smart Contract Wrapper", "sub": "The code that sets how a token behaves", "body": ["The smart contract is the code that issues the token and sets its rules. It does not create the underlying asset or the legal claim, which come from the issuer's legal structure and custody arrangements. What it controls is how the token behaves: who can hold it, how transfers are checked, whether tokens can be frozen or recovered and who can mint or burn them. Two tokens linked to the same asset can behave very differently because of their contracts."], "point": "The contract does not make the claim real. It controls how the token moves, who can hold it and who can stop it.", "chapter": 1, "note": "same gold. different off-switch.", "t": 92.86}], "tagline": "the words people misuse on purpose"}, {"n": 2, "id": "equities", "name": "Tokenized Equities", "lessons": [{"id": "2.1", "page": 9, "title": "Tokenized Equity", "sub": "Company shares, issued or represented onchain", "body": ["A tokenized equity is a token linked to shares in a company. It can be a share issued directly as a token and recorded in the company's register, or a claim linked to shares that a custodian holds for token holders. What backs the token, and who you rely on if something goes wrong, depends on the structure: the issuer, the custodian, any collateral and the legal terms. Tokenizing a share can speed up settlement and widen access. It does not change the company you are exposed to."], "point": "A tokenized share is only as good as its structure. Check what the token represents and who stands behind it.", "chapter": 2, "note": "check what is actually behind it", "t": 111.43}, {"id": "2.2", "page": 10, "title": "Three Types of Tokenized Stock", "sub": "Native, custodial and synthetic tokens are different products", "body": ["Tokenized stocks come in three broad types. With native tokens, the company issues its shares as tokens and holders appear in its share register. With custodial tokens, a custodian holds the shares and the token represents a claim linked to them. Whether holders get ownership rights, votes or a claim if something fails depends on the legal structure. Synthetic tokens track a share's price without a share held for each token. They can be backed by collateral, but your exposure is to the issuer and that collateral, not to the company. Check which type you hold before treating it like a share."], "point": "Native, custodial and synthetic tokens can track the same stock and give you very different rights.", "chapter": 2, "note": "same ticker. three sets of rights.", "t": 120.0}, {"id": "2.3", "page": 11, "title": "The Wrapper Decides Who Can Buy", "sub": "Same share, different rules, different buyers", "body": ["Tokens linked to the same company can be sold under different rules. In the US, Regulation D covers private placements, and who can buy depends on the exemption: Rule 506(c) sales are limited to verified accredited investors, while Rule 506(b) also allows a limited number of sophisticated investors who are not accredited. Securities sold this way are restricted, and resale usually waits for a holding period. Regulation A+ allows sales to the general public, with limits on how much can be raised and an SEC qualification process before sales start. Regulation S covers qualifying offers and sales made outside the United States, with limits on reselling into the US for a set period. Many offerings use more than one route at once."], "point": "Before asking what a token represents, ask which rules it was issued under. They decide who can own it and when it can be resold.", "chapter": 2, "note": "same share. three rulebooks.", "t": 129.29}, {"id": "2.4", "page": 12, "title": "Voting Rights and Corporate Actions", "sub": "Votes and corporate actions are separate problems", "body": ["Two things happen to a share after it is issued. Shareholders may be asked to vote, and corporate actions can change the share itself: dividends, stock splits, tender offers, mergers and buybacks. For token holders, both depend on the issuer or custodian passing information through, collecting holders' choices and updating the token. None of this happens automatically onchain. Some issuers handle votes and corporate actions well. Others pass through votes or dividends but have no clear process for a merger or a tender offer, which can leave token holders waiting while shareholders act."], "point": "A voting feature is easy to advertise. Ask how the issuer will handle the next dividend, split or merger.", "chapter": 2, "note": "merger day. token holders: still waiting.", "t": 137.86}], "tagline": "a stock in a wrapper is still a stock (usually)"}, {"n": 3, "id": "market", "name": "Market Structure", "lessons": [{"id": "3.1", "page": 13, "title": "Secondary Liquidity", "sub": "Owning a token is not the same as being able to sell it", "body": ["Secondary liquidity is your ability to sell what you hold, close to its fair value, in the size you want, when you want. For many tokenized assets it is still limited. Transfer rules restrict who can buy, wallets often need approval before they can receive tokens, and trading is split across venues and chains that do not share order books. The result can be wide spreads, prices that move on small trades and long waits to exit. Compare a token's trading volume with its total value: value shows what was issued, volume shows how much changes hands."], "point": "Liquidity depends less on the token than on how many eligible buyers can reach it, and how quickly.", "chapter": 3, "note": "SELL ▸ … *cricket noises*", "t": 150.0}, {"id": "3.2", "page": 14, "title": "Settlement (T+0 & T+1)", "sub": "Speed is not the same as finality", "body": ["Settlement is the point at which a trade completes: the buyer receives the asset, the seller receives payment and the transfer is final. Most US securities trades settle T+1, one business day after the trade date. T+0 means settlement on the trade date itself, which is same-day, not necessarily instant or atomic. Some tokenized trades are atomic, with the token and the payment moving in a single transaction, but only when both are onchain. If the token moves onchain while the cash goes by bank wire, settlement risk has not gone away. It sits with the payment."], "point": "T+0 promises the same day, not the same second. Ask how the cash side settles before calling a trade instant.", "chapter": 3, "note": "⚡ token: done. 🐌 cash: 'processing'", "t": 158.57}, {"id": "3.3", "page": 15, "title": "Order Books vs AMMs", "sub": "Two ways to set a price, suited to different markets", "body": ["An order book matches buyers and sellers directly. Each side posts a price and a size, and trades happen where they meet. It works well when many participants are trading, which keeps the book full and spreads tight. An automated market maker, or AMM, replaces the matching engine with a pricing formula and a pool of tokens that anyone can trade against. AMMs can keep a market open when trading is thin, but someone has to fund the pool, and a pool holding an asset that rarely trades can leave liquidity providers exposed to out-of-date prices. Many tokenized assets trade only a few times a day."], "point": "Both models work best with steady trading. Many RWAs trade infrequently, which limits how well either one works.", "chapter": 3, "note": "LPs holding stale prices like milk in the sun", "t": 167.14}, {"id": "3.4", "page": 16, "title": "Price Discovery Challenges", "sub": "A price means more when people actually trade at it", "body": ["Price discovery is how a market settles on a price through many trades made close together. Large public stocks trade continuously, so their prices update all day. Many tokenized assets trade far less often, sometimes once a day or less, so the last traded price can be hours or days old. Funds publish a net asset value, and oracles bring that value and other reference prices onchain. These figures are useful, but they are valuations and reported numbers, not prices set by active trading."], "point": "Check when a price was last set by a real trade. For many RWAs, the number you see is a valuation, not a market price.", "chapter": 3, "note": "price: 'current'. last trade: last tuesday.", "t": 175.71}], "tagline": "buying is easy. leaving is the plot twist."}, {"n": 4, "id": "treasuries", "name": "Treasuries", "lessons": [{"id": "4.1", "page": 17, "title": "Tokenized Treasuries", "sub": "Short-term US government debt, held through a token", "body": ["Most tokenized Treasury products are shares in a fund that holds short-term US Treasury bills, often alongside repurchase agreements and cash. The fund issues its shares as tokens, instead of or alongside entries in a traditional register, and buys and holds the bills through the same custody arrangements a conventional fund uses.", "What changes is how shares are held and transferred. Ownership is recorded onchain, and shares can move between approved wallets without waiting for an administrator's next update. Treasury bills are well understood and carry low credit risk, which made them one of the first asset classes to be tokenized at scale."], "point": "Tokenized Treasuries hold the same bills as any Treasury fund. The token changes how shares are recorded and moved.", "chapter": 4, "note": "the T-bill is unbothered", "t": 187.86}, {"id": "4.2", "page": 18, "title": "Who's Building It", "sub": "Managers, issuers and the platforms underneath them", "body": ["Three kinds of firms build tokenized Treasury products. Established asset managers tokenize funds they already run, such as BlackRock's BUIDL and Franklin Templeton's BENJI. Newer, crypto-native issuers such as Ondo and Superstate build funds for onchain distribution and work with partners for custody, administration and compliance. Behind both, tokenization platforms and transfer agents such as Securitize issue the tokens, keep the ownership records and enforce who is allowed to hold them."], "point": "Check whether you hold the fund's own share token or a separate product that holds those shares for you.", "kind": "builders", "builders": [{"name": "BlackRock", "logo": "blackrock"}, {"name": "Franklin Templeton", "logo": "franklin"}, {"name": "Ondo", "logo": "ondo"}, {"name": "Superstate", "logo": "superstate"}, {"name": "Securitize", "logo": "securitize"}], "chapter": 4, "note2": "Examples, not a complete list.", "note": "three roles, many names", "t": 196.43}, {"id": "4.3", "page": 19, "title": "Yield Mechanics", "sub": "How Treasury income reaches a token holder", "body": ["Income on a tokenized Treasury fund comes from the bills and other short-term instruments the fund holds. Interest accrues as they earn and roll over, and products pass it to holders in one of two main ways. Stable-price funds, such as BlackRock's BUIDL and Franklin Templeton's BENJI, keep each token at about one dollar and pay income as additional tokens. Accumulating tokens, such as Ondo's USDY and OUSG, keep the income inside the product, so each token's price rises over time. When holders receive income depends on the product's distribution schedule."], "point": "The token reports the yield. The Treasury bills underneath earn it.", "chapter": 4, "note": "the token takes credit for the group project", "t": 205.0}, {"id": "4.4", "page": 20, "title": "Redemption & Custody Chain", "sub": "Getting cash back means unwinding the same chain in reverse", "body": ["Redemption usually starts with a request through the issuer or its platform. The fund then raises cash, by selling bills or using cash it already holds, while the transfer agent updates the ownership records. The custodian releases the proceeds, and the investor receives cash or stablecoins. Some products burn the token when the request is accepted, others only when the payout is complete. Timing, and when tokens are burned, depend on the product's process. Each step relies on an institution doing its part."], "point": "Burning the token is one step among several. Check each step between your request and the cash arriving.", "chapter": 4, "note": "the 🔥 is the receipt, not the process", "t": 213.57}], "tagline": "the boring asset that proved the point"}, {"n": 5, "id": "yield", "name": "Yield & Returns", "lessons": [{"id": "5.1", "page": 21, "title": "Advertised vs. Realized Yield", "sub": "The number on the page may differ from what you earn", "body": ["Advertised yield is the figure a product shows on its website or app. Realized yield is what a holder actually earns over the time they hold it, and the two may differ. A quoted yield may be historical, such as a 7-day or 30-day figure, or projected, and it may be shown gross or net of fees.", "What you earn also depends on entry and exit costs, when distributions are paid, how much cash the product holds back for redemptions and how the underlying rates move while you hold it."], "point": "Check how the quoted yield is calculated: over what period, and before or after which fees.", "chapter": 5, "note": "18%* *measured on one great tuesday", "t": 232.86}, {"id": "5.2", "page": 22, "title": "Lockups & Redemption Windows", "sub": "Every quoted yield comes with terms for getting your money back", "body": ["Lockups and redemption windows are two different limits on getting your money back. A lockup is a period during which you cannot redeem at all. A redemption window is the schedule on which redemption requests are processed. Many tokenized Treasury funds process requests daily. Private credit and real estate products often redeem monthly or quarterly with a notice period, so a request made today may take weeks to complete. Products that offer faster redemption than their assets allow usually hold extra cash to cover it, which can lower returns."], "point": "Read the redemption terms before the yield. Income only matters if you can get your money out when you need it.", "chapter": 5, "note": "exit opens: next quarter. notice period: also yes.", "t": 241.43}, {"id": "5.3", "page": 23, "title": "Waterfall & Tranching Basics", "sub": "Two tokens can share collateral and carry very different risk", "body": ["A waterfall sets the order in which cash from a pool of assets is paid out. Interest and principal go to the senior tranche first, then to the mezzanine tranche, and whatever is left goes to the junior or equity tranche. Losses run the other way: the junior tranche absorbs them first, and senior holders take losses only after the tranches below them are used up. That is why one loan pool can offer a lower-yield senior token and a higher-yield junior token backed by the same collateral. The extra yield pays for the extra risk."], "point": "The same collateral can carry very different risk. Check which tranche you are buying.", "chapter": 5, "note": "junior gets paid more because junior gets hit first", "t": 250.0}, {"id": "5.4", "page": 24, "title": "Fee Structures", "sub": "Fees and yield are two sides of the same number", "body": ["Tokenized products can charge several kinds of fees. A management fee is charged on the assets under management. A performance fee takes a share of returns above an agreed hurdle. Subscription and redemption fees are charged when you enter or exit and weigh most on short holding periods. Custody, administration, audit and network costs may be bundled into one expense figure or listed separately in the offering documents. Some quoted yields are already net of certain fees and some are not. Check which fees are already included in the quoted yield and which are charged separately."], "point": "Ask for the yield after all fees, then check what is charged on top.", "chapter": 5, "note": "everyone in the chain gets paid. then you.", "t": 259.29}], "tagline": "the number on the billboard vs. the number in your wallet"}, {"n": 6, "id": "credit", "name": "Private Credit", "lessons": [{"id": "6.1", "page": 25, "title": "Tokenized Private Credit", "sub": "The loan stays the same. How you hold it changes.", "body": ["Private credit is lending that happens outside public bond markets. A fund or a direct lender makes loans to businesses, holds them and collects interest until they are repaid. Tokenizing private credit means issuing tokens that represent a claim on a loan, a pool of loans or a fund that holds them.", "The token carries the loans' economics without changing them. Borrowers still have repayment schedules, the loans keep their terms, and someone still has to assess each borrower's credit before any money is lent."], "point": "Tokenizing a loan changes how you hold it. It does not change who owes the money.", "chapter": 6, "note": "now with a token. still a loan.", "t": 272.14}, {"id": "6.2", "page": 26, "title": "Who's Building It", "sub": "Platforms in this sector do different jobs", "body": ["Firms in onchain private credit play different roles. Figure originates loans, such as home equity lines of credit, and records them onchain. Maple underwrites loans and lends to institutional borrowers through its own lending pools. Centrifuge provides infrastructure that asset managers and lenders use to tokenize loans and funds and to run their pools onchain. When a borrower stops paying, these roles decide who manages the recovery and who takes the loss."], "point": "Find out who underwrote the loan and who manages it now. That may not be the firm that sold you the token.", "kind": "builders", "builders": [{"name": "Figure", "logo": "figure"}, {"name": "Maple", "logo": "maple"}, {"name": "Centrifuge", "logo": "centrifuge"}], "chapter": 6, "note2": "Examples, not a complete list.", "note": "the seller isn't always the underwriter", "t": 280.71}, {"id": "6.3", "page": 27, "title": "Yield Structure & Underwriting", "sub": "A rate is only as good as the credit work behind it", "body": ["Yield in private credit comes from borrowers paying interest on real loans. The advertised rate starts from what borrowers agreed to pay. What reaches token holders is that interest minus servicing costs, fees and losses on loans that stop performing. Underwriting is the work behind the rate. It decides who can borrow, what collateral secures each loan and how much margin sits between the collateral's value and the amount lent. A rate well above similar pools usually reflects more risk, not better lending."], "point": "Look at the fees, the collateral and the default history before the headline rate.", "chapter": 6, "note": "they looked. they passed.", "t": 289.29}, {"id": "6.4", "page": 28, "title": "Redemption Terms & Liquidity Reality", "sub": "A redemption schedule depends on cash being available", "body": ["Most private credit tokens offer redemptions on a schedule, often weekly, monthly or quarterly. The schedule describes the manager's intent. It is not a guarantee, because the loans in the pool do not mature on the same timetable. If redemption requests outpace repayments and new money, the manager may draw on cash reserves, then use gates that delay or cap withdrawals. Evergreen funds and fixed-term funds handle this differently, and the difference shows most in stressed markets. Check whether the redemption terms have ever been tested, and what happened."], "point": "A redemption schedule is a policy, not a promise. Check how it held up when many investors wanted out.", "chapter": 6, "note": "window: open* *until everyone leaves at once", "t": 297.86}], "tagline": "somebody still has to pay the loan back"}, {"n": 7, "id": "realestate", "name": "Real Estate", "lessons": [{"id": "7.1", "page": 29, "title": "Tokenized Real Estate", "sub": "The building stays put. The ownership record moves.", "body": ["Real estate is hard to divide and slow to sell. To tokenize a property, a sponsor usually places it in a legal entity, such as an LLC, another special purpose vehicle or a trust, which holds the title. Tokens then represent interests in that entity. In most structures the token is a claim on the entity that owns the building, not on the building itself.", "Someone still has to hold title, manage the property, collect rent and handle the legal work that comes with owning real estate."], "point": "Tokenizing a property changes how you hold your stake. It does not change what the entity owns or who manages it.", "chapter": 7, "note": "can't fit a house in a wallet. we checked.", "t": 310.0}, {"id": "7.2", "page": 30, "title": "Who's Building It", "sub": "Four builders, four different things a token can hold", "body": ["Tokenized real estate platforms are not building the same product. Lofty fractionalizes US rental homes: each property sits in its own LLC, and tokens represent membership interests in that LLC. Propy works on the property transaction. Its model can tie an NFT to an LLC that owns a property, so transferring the NFT transfers the LLC and the property with it. Blocksquare provides technology that other operators use to issue property tokens, which give holders contractual economic rights, such as a share of rental income, rather than ownership of the property. Balcony works with county record offices, such as Bergen County in New Jersey, to put deeds and title history onchain."], "point": "An LLC interest, a contractual right and a public title record are different things. Check which one your token represents.", "kind": "builders", "builders": [{"name": "Lofty", "logo": "lofty"}, {"name": "Propy", "logo": "propy"}, {"name": "Blocksquare", "logo": "blocksquare"}, {"name": "Balcony", "logo": "balcony"}], "chapter": 7, "note": "same asset class. four answers.", "t": 318.57}, {"id": "7.3", "page": 31, "title": "Fractional Ownership Mechanics", "sub": "How a house becomes units, and how units become tokens", "body": ["In most tokenized real estate, you don't own the building directly. You own part of the entity that owns it. A sponsor buys the property and places it in a special purpose vehicle, often an LLC, which holds the title. Ownership of that entity is divided into units, and each token maps to a unit. As a simplified example, 1,000 tokens issued against a $250,000 house would each represent $250 of the entity's value, before costs and fees. Rent goes to the entity, the operator pays management, maintenance, insurance and property tax, and the rest is distributed to holders on a set schedule. Your rights as a holder, including any voting rights and whether holders can force a sale, are set out in the operating agreement."], "point": "Read the operating agreement, not just the listing. It sets the fees, the payout schedule and what say you have over the property.", "flow": ["PROPERTY", "SPV (E.G. AN LLC)", "UNITS", "TOKENS"], "chapter": 7, "note": "your token won't open the front door", "t": 327.14}, {"id": "7.4", "page": 32, "title": "The Liquidity Reality Check", "sub": "Faster transfers, but buyers are still limited", "body": ["Tokenization can make the mechanics of an exit easier. Selling a stake in a private property deal has traditionally involved lawyers, brokers, amended agreements and weeks or months of waiting. A token transfer can settle in minutes and update the ownership record at the same time. What tokenization does not provide on its own is a deep pool of buyers. Transfer restrictions limit who can buy, Reg D offerings carry holding periods, and many platforms only match trades between their own verified users, so tokens can trade below their stated value. On platforms that accept them as collateral, holders can also borrow against their tokens instead of selling."], "point": "Compare the exit with a traditional private property sale, then check how many buyers can take the other side.", "flow": ["SELL", "BORROW AGAINST IT", "HOLD TO EXIT"], "chapter": 7, "note": "faster rails. still need buyers.", "t": 336.43}], "tagline": "you can't put a house in a wallet"}, {"n": 8, "id": "rails", "name": "Infrastructure & Compliance Rails", "lessons": [{"id": "8.1", "page": 33, "title": "Oracles", "sub": "Bringing outside data onchain", "body": ["A blockchain only knows what has been written to it. It cannot see the price of a Treasury bill, a fund's net asset value or a property valuation. An oracle is a service that brings that kind of offchain information onchain, where smart contracts can use it.", "For RWAs, oracles report reference values such as prices, net asset values, interest rates and reserve data. A tokenized fund may need its NAV published onchain, and a lending market that accepts tokenized collateral needs a price to set loan limits and trigger liquidations. Contracts act on whatever the oracle reports, so the quality of that data matters."], "point": "An oracle reports a value. Check where the value comes from and how often it is updated.", "chapter": 8, "note": "the contract believes everything. zero judgment.", "t": 348.57}, {"id": "8.2", "page": 34, "title": "Bridges", "sub": "A token can cross chains. The claim behind it may not come along.", "body": ["A bridge moves a token from one blockchain to another. Many bridges lock the original token on the first chain and mint a representation of it on the second, so what arrives is a claim on the locked token, not the token itself. For RWAs this matters, because the issuer recognizes the holders of the tokens on its own register. Some issuers now issue their tokens natively on several chains from a single register, so holders are recognized on each chain. Where that is not the case, the bridge adds another party, and another risk, between you and the asset."], "point": "A bridged token is a claim on a claim. Check which version the issuer will redeem.", "chapter": 8, "note": "a claim on a claim on a… you get it", "t": 357.14}, {"id": "8.3", "page": 35, "title": "KYC / AML Onchain", "sub": "Compliance still applies once an asset is tokenized", "body": ["Know Your Customer (KYC) and Anti-Money Laundering (AML) checks are the identity and source-of-funds work a regulated issuer has to do, and tokenization does not remove them. The investor is verified offchain and linked to an approved wallet. The token contract can then check that approval before a transfer goes through, so a transfer to an unapproved wallet fails. Ongoing AML monitoring, such as sanctions screening and reviews of unusual activity, still happens offchain. The trade-off is that a permissioned token can only move between wallets the issuer has approved."], "point": "Some compliance checks now run in the token's transfer rules. Identity checks and ongoing monitoring still happen offchain.", "chapter": 8, "note": "no awkward email weeks later. it just fails.", "t": 365.71}, {"id": "8.4", "page": 36, "title": "Accredited Investor Gating", "sub": "Verification asks who you are. Accreditation asks whether you qualify.", "body": ["In the US, accreditation decides who can buy many private offerings. An individual generally qualifies with income above $200,000, or $300,000 together with a spouse or partner, in each of the past two years, with the same expected this year. Another route is a net worth above $1 million, excluding the main home. Holders of certain professional credentials, such as the Series 7, 65 or 82 licenses, also qualify. Onchain, accredited status can be stored as one more attribute on the allowlist that already holds an investor's KYC approval. Gating by wealth keeps some private offerings out of reach for most people, and the RWA Foundation supports opening access more widely."], "point": "The technology can already check who qualifies. The rules that decide who qualifies are what still limit access.", "chapter": 8, "note": "everyone else: please enjoy the lobby", "t": 374.29}, {"id": "8.5", "page": 37, "title": "Jurisdiction Arbitrage", "sub": "The token travels anywhere. The rules behind it come from one place.", "body": ["Tokens can be transferred globally, but securities law is national, so every issuer chooses a home jurisdiction. Delaware, the Cayman Islands, Liechtenstein, Singapore and Abu Dhabi each set different rules on who can buy, what must be disclosed and which courts hear disputes. Often this is ordinary structuring, such as using an offshore vehicle to serve investors outside the US. Sometimes it is a search for the lightest rules available. A product's marketing page rarely tells you which."], "point": "Check where a product is based before you look at its yield. That decides which rules protect you and which court hears a claim.", "chapter": 8, "note": "same 8%. very different court.", "t": 382.86}], "tagline": "the plumbing nobody tweets about"}, {"n": 9, "id": "commodities", "name": "Commodities", "lessons": [{"id": "9.1", "page": 38, "title": "Tokenized Commodities", "sub": "The metal stays in the vault. The claim on it moves.", "body": ["A tokenized commodity is a claim on a physical commodity that someone else holds. Gold is the most common example. A custodian stores the metal in a vault, auditors or inspectors check that it is there, and each token represents a stated amount of it. Silver and other commodities work in a similar way.", "The token does not change the commodity. It changes how a share of it can be held and moved: an amount of gold that once needed a broker and a vault account can sit in a wallet. Storage, audits and redemption still happen offchain."], "point": "A commodity token moves the claim, not the metal. Its value depends on the vault and the terms behind it.", "chapter": 9, "note": "the gold is not coming with you", "t": 401.43}, {"id": "9.2", "page": 39, "title": "Who's Building It", "sub": "Mostly gold, and the vault matters more than the ticker", "body": ["The tokenized commodities market is mostly gold. Examples include Paxos with PAX Gold, Tether with Tether Gold and Matrixdock with XAUm. Each token represents a claim on gold held with a custodian. Vault operators and refiners store and handle the metal, and auditors check that the bars are there. Issuers differ most in how holders can redeem: for cash, for physical gold or both."], "point": "A gold token is only as good as its vault. Check who audits it and how you can redeem.", "kind": "builders", "builders": [{"name": "PAX Gold", "logo": "paxg"}, {"name": "Tether Gold", "logo": "xaut"}, {"name": "Matrixdock", "logo": "matrixdock"}], "chapter": 9, "note2": "Examples, not a complete list. Silver, platinum and other commodities are tokenized too.", "note": "the vault matters more than the ticker", "t": 410.0}, {"id": "9.3", "page": 40, "title": "Custody & Redemption", "sub": "The token moves in seconds. The metal moves on the custodian's terms.", "body": ["The first question is whether the metal is allocated. Allocated gold means specific bars, identified by serial number, are set aside for token holders, typically in vaults holding LBMA Good Delivery bars and run by firms such as Brink's or Malca-Amit. Unallocated holdings are generally a claim against the provider rather than ownership of specific bars, so if the provider fails you may rank alongside its other creditors. Insurance and physical redemption terms vary by issuer. Many holders can redeem for cash, while physical delivery may require a minimum amount, full KYC and paying for shipping."], "point": "Custody is about who holds the bars. Redemption is about whether, and how, you can get them.", "chapter": 9, "note2": "Independent bar counts are what make these claims checkable.", "note": "bring a wheelbarrow", "t": 418.57}], "tagline": "it's mostly gold. ask about the vault."}, {"n": 10, "id": "funds", "name": "Funds", "lessons": [{"id": "10.1", "page": 41, "title": "Tokenized Funds", "sub": "The fund keeps its rules. The shares get new rails.", "body": ["A tokenized fund is a regular fund whose shares are issued or recorded as tokens. The manager still runs the portfolio, the administrator still calculates the net asset value and the transfer agent still keeps the register, with the share records kept onchain. Money market funds are a common example.", "Tokenizing a fund leaves the strategy unchanged and changes how shares are issued, transferred and settled. Subscriptions and redemptions can settle faster, and shares can be held in a wallet or, where allowed, used as collateral. The fund's legal structure and its regulator stay the same."], "point": "A fund token is a fund share on new rails. What it is worth depends on the fund behind it.", "chapter": 10, "note": "money market funds: a common example", "t": 430.71}, {"id": "10.2", "page": 42, "title": "Who's Building It", "sub": "Who does what in a tokenized fund", "body": ["Firms building tokenized funds fall into three broad groups. Established asset managers issue tokenized share classes of funds they already run. Crypto-native firms launch funds designed for onchain distribution from the start. Tokenization platforms and transfer agents support both, issuing the tokens and keeping the record of who owns each share."], "point": "Before you buy, check who keeps the official record of who owns the shares.", "kind": "builders", "groups": [{"label": "ASSET MANAGERS", "items": [{"name": "BlackRock", "logo": "blackrock"}, {"name": "Franklin Templeton", "logo": "franklin"}, {"name": "Apollo", "logo": "apollo"}]}, {"label": "CRYPTO-NATIVE", "items": [{"name": "Ondo", "logo": "ondo"}, {"name": "Superstate", "logo": "superstate"}, {"name": "Spiko", "logo": "spiko"}]}, {"label": "PLATFORMS", "items": [{"name": "Securitize", "logo": "securitize"}, {"name": "Centrifuge", "logo": "centrifuge2"}, {"name": "Libeara", "logo": "libeara"}]}], "chapter": 10, "note2": "Examples, not a complete list.", "note": "a simple guide to who does what", "t": 439.29}, {"id": "10.3", "page": 43, "title": "Yield & Redemption", "sub": "How a tokenized fund pays you, and how you get out", "body": ["The yield on a tokenized fund comes from the assets the fund holds, not from the token. Redemption is the other half: returning your shares to the fund for cash. Selling to another buyer can happen whenever a buyer is available. Redemption happens on the fund's schedule."], "point": "Selling a token can be quick. Redeeming it for cash follows the fund's schedule.", "sections": [{"h": "WHERE THE YIELD COMES FROM", "t": "Treasury bills and loans pay interest, and property earns rent. The token is a claim on that income, not its source."}, {"h": "HOW THE YIELD REACHES YOU", "t": "Some funds pay income as additional tokens and keep the price steady. Others keep the income in the fund, so each token's price rises. The income is the same; the mechanics differ."}, {"h": "HOW YOU GET YOUR CASH BACK", "t": "You submit a redemption request. The fund uses cash or sells assets, cancels your tokens and pays you. Some funds do this daily, others monthly or quarterly."}], "chapter": 10, "note": "sell: now. redeem: when the fund says so.", "t": 447.86}, {"id": "10.4", "page": 44, "title": "Open vs Closed-End", "sub": "Two fund structures, two ways out", "body": ["An open-end fund issues new shares when investors subscribe and redeems shares when they leave. A closed-end fund generally does not redeem on demand, so investors usually exit by selling to someone else. The fund's assets usually decide which structure makes sense."], "point": "Open-end funds redeem on request. Closed-end funds generally don't. A token does not change which one you hold.", "sections": [{"h": "HOW AN OPEN-END FUND WORKS", "t": "Shares are issued and redeemed at net asset value, typically every business day. Money market and Treasury funds work this way because their assets are easy to sell."}, {"h": "HOW A CLOSED-END FUND WORKS", "t": "Shares are not redeemed on demand. Some closed-end funds have a fixed term and a single raise; others raise more over time or offer periodic repurchases, as interval and tender-offer funds do. Private credit and property often use these structures."}, {"h": "WHAT THE TOKEN CHANGES", "t": "A token can make closed-end shares easier to transfer without making them redeemable. Unless the fund offers a repurchase, you exit by selling to another buyer."}], "chapter": 10, "note": "tradable ≠ redeemable", "t": 456.43}], "tagline": "same fund. faster pipes."}, {"n": 11, "id": "risk", "name": "Risk & Red Flags", "lessons": [{"id": "11.1", "page": 45, "title": "Why Self-Reported Metrics Get Flagged", "sub": "A number is only as reliable as whoever checked it", "body": ["A self-reported metric is any figure a project publishes about itself, such as assets, holders, yield or volume. Publishing these is normal. The issue is that nobody independent has checked them, and the project has reasons to present them well.", "RWAs make this harder to judge. Token supply can be read onchain, but the assets behind it are held offchain. A headline figure might describe a live fund, a signed mandate or a pipeline that has not closed. A flag tells you whether you are looking at checked data or the issuer's own claim."], "point": "A flag does not mean a number is false. It means nobody outside the issuer has checked it.", "chapter": 11, "note": "source: trust me bro", "t": 475.0}, {"id": "11.2", "page": 46, "title": "What Verified Means", "sub": "Audits, attestations and proof of reserves check different things", "body": ["In RWA, 'verified' can mean several different checks, and each one covers a defined scope. A round number with no report behind it is none of them. Before relying on the word, ask which check was done, what it covered and when."], "point": "Ask who did the check, what it covered and for which date or period.", "sections": [{"h": "AUDIT", "t": "An independent auditor examines financial statements against accounting standards and gives an opinion. It covers the period and the entities in scope, not everything about a product."}, {"h": "ATTESTATION", "t": "An accountant reports on specific claims made by management, such as whether reserves covered the tokens in issue. Some cover a single date, others a period. Read what was tested."}, {"h": "PROOF OF RESERVES", "t": "Shows that assets matching the token supply exist, sometimes updated continuously through an oracle. On its own it does not show liabilities, so it does not establish solvency."}], "chapter": 11, "note": "checked by whom? covering what?", "t": 483.57}, {"id": "11.3", "page": 47, "title": "Who Holds the Keys", "sub": "Admin keys decide who can mint, freeze or upgrade your token", "body": ["For many asset-backed tokens, securing the underlying asset is only part of the picture. The other part is the smart contract and who controls its admin permissions. Those permissions can change what happens to your token after you buy it."], "point": "Multisig controls and timelocks act as brakes. Ask who holds the keys and what limits them.", "sections": [{"h": "MINT", "t": "Creates new tokens. Total supply should match the required backing, though not every mint needs a new deposit. If one wallet can mint without checks, supply can outgrow the assets."}, {"h": "FREEZE", "t": "Pauses transfers or blocks an address, typically for sanctions or court orders. It is a compliance tool, and it can apply to your wallet too."}, {"h": "UPGRADE", "t": "If the contract is upgradeable, the code behind the token can change while its address stays the same. Fixes can ship quickly, and so can new rules."}], "chapter": 11, "note": "admin key: one guy named dave", "t": 492.86}, {"id": "11.4", "page": 48, "title": "The Trust Chain", "sub": "Every link between you and the asset is someone you rely on", "body": ["A token is a claim, and a claim is only as good as whoever has to honor it. Moving onchain removes some intermediaries, but not all of them. If one of the remaining parties fails, a token can stay onchain and still lose value."], "point": "Check who you rely on at each step, then ask what happens if one of them fails.", "sections": [{"h": "ISSUER", "t": "Owes you redemption. Holding the assets in a bankruptcy-remote SPV can limit your exposure if the issuer becomes insolvent, but it does not guarantee protection."}, {"h": "CUSTODIAN", "t": "Holds the backing assets. If it can lend or re-pledge them, known as rehypothecation, someone else may also have a claim on them."}, {"h": "RAILS", "t": "Oracles report reference values, bridges move tokens and transfer agents keep the register. Any of them can be wrong, offline or compromised."}], "chapter": 11, "note": "onchain ≠ okay", "t": 501.43}], "tagline": "the part they skip in the pitch deck"}], "herd": {"page": 49, "eyebrow": "THE HERD", "title": "RWA Bulls", "sub": "Forty-eight pages of reading done. Now go follow these twelve.", "point": "The book stops at page 49. The herd keeps moving.", "accounts": [{"name": "RWA Foundation", "handle": "RWAFoundation_"}, {"name": "Happy", "handle": "happytokenizing"}, {"name": "Zeus", "handle": "ZeusRWA"}, {"name": "Ray Buckton", "handle": "RayBuckton"}, {"name": "Scofield", "handle": "ScofieldOnchain"}, {"name": "Diego", "handle": "diegoxyz"}, {"name": "stein", "handle": "steinRWA"}, {"name": "Harry Tran", "handle": "HarryTran_RWA"}, {"name": "Zinn", "handle": "zinnresearch"}, {"name": "RWA Llama", "handle": "RwaLlama"}, {"name": "incept1oN", "handle": "_incept1on"}, {"name": "Pavel Yurich", "handle": "PavelYuRichRWA"}]}, "pages": 49, "version": 2};
